Phenylketonuria
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Musty/mousy urine odour, Severe intellectual disability (untreated), Hypopigmentation/fair skin, Elevated serum phenylalanine

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-038
Reason for Visit: Acute behavioral crisis, severe irritability, and new-onset tremors.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 16-year-old patient with a known history of poorly controlled Phenylketonuria presenting with a 48-hour history of severe, escalating irritability, insomnia, and new-onset continuous hand tremors. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the high risk of acute neurotoxicity from uncontrolled phenylalanine levels, requiring immediate clinical evaluation, crisis de-escalation, and initiation of a strict metabolic washout protocol.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He hasn't slept in two days, he is completely erratic, and his hands are shaking uncontrollably.
History of Present Illness: The parents report a 48-hour history of an acute behavioral and neurological decline. The patient has a known diagnosis of PKU. Recently, the patient has been highly non-compliant with his strict, low-phenylalanine medical diet, consuming large amounts of standard dietary protein. Consequently, he has developed severe hyperactivity, profound irritability, and severe behavioural problems that are unmanageable at home. Over the past 12 hours, the parents noted the onset of extrapyramidal motor signs, specifically a continuous resting tremor in both hands. The mother reports a very strong, distinctive musty or mousy odour to his breath and skin.
Functional Status: Ambulant, but currently exhibiting severely impaired judgment and erratic behavior.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of permanent neurological damage if neurotoxic levels are not rapidly reduced; caregiver burnout.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 60.0 Kg
General: Patient is highly agitated, pacing the room, and displays obvious hypopigmentation (very fair skin, blonde hair, blue eyes). A distinct musty odor permeates the room.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals a continuous, fine resting tremor in the bilateral upper extremities. Mild dystonic posturing of the hands is occasionally noted during pacing. Deep tendon reflexes are hyperactive (3+) globally. No focal motor weakness. The patient exhibits severe inattention and a flight of ideas, making formal cognitive assessment difficult. Skin exam reveals a chronic, severe eczematous skin rash on the flexor surfaces of the arms and behind the knees, exacerbated by scratching.
Environmental Observations: Empty fast-food wrappers (high protein) are visible in the patient's bedroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has completely refused his prescribed amino acid medical formula supplement for the past two weeks.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Phenylketonuria (with acute neurotoxicity secondary to dietary non-compliance) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Illicit Substance Intoxication (e.g., Amphetamines) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of the pathognomonic musty odor, the specific extrapyramidal signs (tremors/dystonia), and the known, documented history of dietary non-compliance perfectly explaining the presentation.

Primary Bipolar Mania - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause, as the severe agitation and insomnia are directly driven by elevated serum phenylalanine acting as a neurotoxin, rather than a primary psychiatric disorder.

Essential Tremor - Ruled out/unlikely given the sudden, acute onset correlating with the behavioral crisis, the presence of hyperreflexia, and the systemic metabolic context.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Dietary non-compliance in an adolescent)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of severe irritability, insomnia, and extrapyramidal tremors in the context of dietary non-compliance strongly indicated acute phenylalanine neurotoxicity. The objective findings of hyperreflexia, a resting tremor, and the pathognomonic musty odor confirmed a dangerous accumulation of the amino acid. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because sustained high levels of phenylalanine cause irreversible demyelination and intellectual decline. Immediate medical intervention was required to safely administer acute anxiolysis to break the behavioral crisis and to enforce an immediate return to the specialized medical formula to initiate metabolic clearance.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Diazepam 5mg to rapidly reduce the severe acute agitation and facilitate rest. Prescribed a short course of topical hydrocortisone for the severe eczematous rash to remove a secondary source of agitation. Supervised the immediate consumption of a large dose of the prescribed phenylalanine-free amino acid formula.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you drink your medical formula immediately upon waking before doing anything else, you ensure your brain gets the safe proteins it needs; therefore, building this strict morning habit protects your ability to focus and control your mood all day. When the parents remove all high-protein snacks from the house entirely, the temptation is eliminated; maintaining a completely "safe" food environment prevents impulsive cheating.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the specialized metabolic dietetics team for a crisis review.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A.
Follow-up: GP Telehealth review in 24 hours to ensure sleep has occurred and strict dietary compliance is maintained; urgent blood draw ordered for tomorrow morning to quantify phenylalanine levels.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient experiences a seizure, if the tremors become so severe he cannot hold a cup, or if he becomes aggressively violent towards others.

